{"kind": "authoring.reviewed", "at": "2026-09-13T23:32:20.496153+00:00", "summary": "0 outcome(s) added, 4 checkpoint concern(s)", "applied": [], "rejected": [], "concerns": ["s1: The checkpoint is an OR whose second branch, 'button named \"Search\" is present', is far too weak to identify the Member Search screen: a Search button appears on virtually every list/lookup screen in this application, so the step would pass on any wrong screen the session happened to land on. The URL branch alone is the meaningful assertion; the OR effectively disables it.", "s2: No checkpoint at all on the step that types the member id. If the field is not focused, is cleared by a re-render, or retains a prior value, the flow silently searches for the wrong id and every later step still passes - including reading a balance that belongs to a different member. This step should assert the entered value is present in the id input before the search is run.", "s3: Matching only the URL /members/search proves the search was submitted but says nothing about what came back. The same URL is reached both for a successful hit and for the 'No records found matching your search criteria.' case, so this checkpoint cannot distinguish the success path from the MEMBER_NOT_FOUND outcome. The not-found alert must be evaluated at this point, before s4, or the outcome will be mis-reported.", "s4: On the not-found path the flow never reaches a /members/<id> URL, so this checkpoint fails and the run is reported as broken automation rather than as the MEMBER_NOT_FOUND business outcome. Outcome detection has to take precedence over this checkpoint. Separately, the checkpoint only proves a member detail URL was reached - it does not confirm the record belongs to the requested id, nor that a savings balance is actually rendered, so a partially loaded or wrong member page would still pass."]}
